    "growth_divergence_warning": "Equity 3yr CAGR (17.0%) diverges >30% from revenue 3yr CAGR (6.5%). Book value is not a usable growth proxy here: TJX returns essentially all FCF via buybacks and dividends, so equity growth reflects margin recovery and retained-vs-returned mix, not reinvestment. Revenue CAGR from FY2023 is used instead.",
    "notes": "5-year windows start at FY2021 (COVID store closures: revenue $32.1B, EPS $0.08), so revenue 5yr 13.4% is a rebound artefact and EPS 5yr is meaningless (null). Owner-FCF series (reported FCF - SBC - est. equity-award taxes - interest income): FY2021 ~$3,893M (working-capital release), FY2023 ~$2,397M, FY2026 ~$4,458M; 3yr 23% reflects margin recovery from the FY2022-23 freight/inventory trough, not a sustainable rate. SBC (~0.35% of revenue) is small and growing roughly in line with revenue, so SBC-adjusted and reported FCF CAGRs barely differ."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "The off-price share-gain story continues at its historical pace: comps 2-3%, ~4% annual store growth from FY2028 toward the raised 7,500-store target, HomeGoods and TK Maxx International growing slightly faster than Marmaxx. Tariff costs are absorbed through opportunistic buying and pricing; the Q2 FY2027 Marmaxx merchandise-mix miss is a one-quarter execution slip. Increased marketing spend and wage inflation consume most of the merchandise-margin and occupancy leverage, so EBIT margin rises only ~50bp over a decade to ~12.4%. Capex runs ~3.6% of sales during the store acceleration, then eases.",
    "bull": "Department-store and full-price closures plus abundant vendor overstock (tariff-disrupted supply chains push more goods into off-price) let TJX run 3-4% comps on top of 4%+ store growth; Spain and other new markets work and HomeGoods/Homesense take home-decor share. Marketing investment converts into traffic, and merchandise margin plus scale leverage take EBIT margin to ~13.6% (pretax ~14%), above anything TJX has printed. Exit at 22x terminal owner FCF is consistent with the premium the market already pays for the franchise.",
    "bear": "A US consumer downturn hits discretionary apparel/home in FY2028 while tariffs and retaliatory measures raise landed costs; Ross and Burlington price more aggressively, Marmaxx execution errors recur, and vendor inventory discipline tightens off-price supply. Comps go flat-to-negative for a year, store growth is slowed to protect returns, and EBIT margin falls back to ~10.8% (FY2023-24 levels) and stays there as wage costs and the new marketing budget prove sticky. Off-price still trades customers down, so revenue does not shrink - the bear is a margin and multiple story (16x exit), not a solvency one."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "TJX is a mature, high-return compounder whose value rests on durable off-price share gains and a ~12% EBIT margin, not on growth optionality. The valuation is an owner-FCF DCF at an 8.5% USD WACC: SBC fully expensed (immaterial at ~0.36% of sales), interest income on the cash pile stripped because net cash is added in the bridge, operating-lease rent kept inside EBITDA with lease liabilities excluded from net debt. Buybacks are value-neutral in the model; they are how TJX returns the owner FCF.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Margin mean reversion from a record FY2027 pretax margin (12.3-12.4%) - every 100bp of EBITDA margin is roughly 8-9% of value",
      "Tariff and trade-policy cost on merchandise margin (FY2026 10-K high-severity risk)",
      "Store-growth acceleration to ~4%/yr toward 7,500 stores may cannibalise or earn lower returns in new markets (Spain)",
      "Equity-award withholding taxes are estimated (~$50M) since the XBRL tag ended in FY2023 - immaterial to value",
      "Terminal-value heavy: a 10-year horizon on a mature retailer means ~2/3 of EV sits in the terminal value"
    ]
  },
